---
import { Code } from "astro:components"
import { theme } from "../code.ts"
import CodePanel from "../instrument/CodePanel.astro"
import Instrument from "../instrument/Instrument.astro"
import Layout from "../Layout.astro"

const title = "Calder, a CI/CD pipeline that is a program"
const description =
  "Calder is CI/CD you write in TypeScript. The compiler checks what each step may do, a plan shows what any event runs, and every result is kept by what it read."

const forbidden = `const deploy = Action.make("deploy", {
  needs: { site },
  after: [typecheck, test],
  grants: { secrets: ["deploy-token"] },
}, …)

On.pullRequest([typecheck, test, site, deploy])`

const ungranted = `const deploy = Action.make("deploy", {
  needs: { site },
  after: [typecheck, test],
}, function*({ site }) {
  yield* Deploy.publish(site) // reads Secret.value("deploy-token")
})

On.push("main", [deploy])`

const planTest = `import { expect, it } from "@effect/vitest"
import { Calder, Event } from "@calder-ci/core"
import { Effect } from "effect"
import project from "./ci.ts"

it.effect("never deploys a pull request", () =>
  Effect.gen(function*() {
    const event = Event.pullRequest({ number: 7 })
    const plan = yield* Calder.plan(project, event)
    const names = plan.steps.map((step) => step.name)
    expect(names).not.toContain("deploy")
  }))`

const before = `$ calder check
  # runs the working copy, jj or git, like a pull request

$ calder watch ~/work
  # checks every jj workspace below ~/work in the background,
  # once its snapshot has stayed the same for two minutes`
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <link slot="head" rel="canonical" href={Astro.site} />

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-text">
      <h1>Your pipeline is a program.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Calder is CI/CD you write in TypeScript, for repositories with a Nix flake. The compiler checks
        what each step may do. A plan shows what any event runs before anything runs. And Calder keeps
        every result by exactly what it read, so nothing runs twice.
      </p>
      <p class="note">An experiment: one installation, an API that changes most weeks, no release yet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bench">
      <Instrument mode="plan" label="The example pipeline, played" />
      <CodePanel />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="types">
    <div class="section-text">
      <h2>The compiler knows what a step may do</h2>
      <p>
        A step that deploys or reads a secret says so in its grants, and the grants are part of its
        type. A pull-request rule takes only steps without grants, so handing it
        the <code>deploy</code> step doesn't compile. Neither does a step that reads a secret nobody
        granted.
      </p>
      <p class="aside">
        The planner checks again when it runs, for pipelines nothing type-checked. It also keeps tasks
        that read secrets or hold the deploy lease out of pull-request rules; those have no grants for
        the compiler to see.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="errors">
      <figure class="error-card">
        <figcaption>Deploy on pull requests</figcaption>
        <Code code={forbidden} lang="ts" theme={theme} />
        <p class="error-text"><span>error TS2322</span> Type '&lbrace; readonly secrets: readonly ["deploy-token"]; &rbrace;' is not assignable to type 'never'.</p>
      </figure>
      <figure class="error-card">
        <figcaption>A secret nobody granted</figcaption>
        <Code code={ungranted} lang="ts" theme={theme} />
        <p class="error-text"><span>error TS2322</span> Type 'Secret&lt;"deploy-token"&gt;' is not assignable to type 'Base'.</p>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-split" id="reuse">
    <div class="section-text">
      <h2>It keeps every result by what it read</h2>
      <p>
        A task's key is the git tree ids of the files it reads, the key of the setup it starts from,
        its Nix dev shell and its command. Git hashes every file already, so a key needs no pass over
        the files. When a key has a result, from main, a pull request or an agent's working copy, the
        step settles at once.
      </p>
      <p>
        With <code>Vitest.files</code>, each test file gets a key of its own that follows its imports.
        When the same key gives a different outcome than before, Calder says so: either the key misses
        an input, or the test is flaky.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Instrument mode="edits" label="What a pull request reruns after an edit" />
  </section>

  <section class="section section-split" id="before">
    <div class="section-text">
      <h2>It runs before you push</h2>
      <p>
        The command <code>calder check</code> runs your working copy like a pull request, before you
        push it. The command <code>calder watch</code> does the same in the background for every jj
        workspace below a directory, so agents working in parallel get checked while they work.
      </p>
      <p>
        Calder keeps their results by key like any other. The pull request that follows finds them, and
        so does main after the merge.
      </p>
    </div>
    <pre class="terminal"><code>{before}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-split" id="test">
    <div class="section-text">
      <h2>You can test it like any program</h2>
      <p>
        The function <code>Calder.plan</code> takes a pipeline and an event and returns the steps that
        run, without running anything. Write tests for the rules that matter to you, with vitest. On
        the command line, <code>calder plan</code> prints the same plan for any event.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="code-block">
      <p class="code-file">.calder/ci.test.ts</p>
      <Code code={planTest} lang="ts" theme={theme} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section status" id="status">
    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>
      Calder is an experiment by Hauke Schnau. It has one installation, its API changes most weeks,
      and there is no release yet. A repository needs a Nix flake: tasks run in its dev shells, and
      builds are its packages.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<script>
  import "../instrument/client.ts"
</script>
